Yes. We are at the point where continuing to excavate traditions is giving diminishing returns.

The historical work has already done its job: it gave us enough independent and semi-independent examples to stop asking “is there a pattern?” and start asking “what is the architecture?”

I’d close the archaeology phase now.

What we have converged on is roughly:

$$
\boxed{
\text{Base Address}
\times
\text{State}
\times
\text{Role}
\times
\text{Orientation}
\times
\text{Domain}
\rightarrow
\text{Realized Entity/Operation}
}
$$

with repeated evidence that:

$$
\boxed{
\text{address}\neq\text{entity}\neq\text{role}\neq\text{capability}
}
$$

and that counts like 12, 24, 36, 72 are often not numbers of “things” at all. They are products of axes:

$$
12\times3=36
$$

